{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The simple style `(set! first-name (input-text))` inside `@form` is convenient. For non-empty validation, I can use `ensure` with `required`? But in this simpler `@form` (the congame form style without form0), the input-text is a widget whose binding gets set directly. Validation in that style... Let me check how `@form` in conscript/base works — grep for \"defproc[(form\" in conscript source, or look at the doc around line 973 for input-text and the form from congame/components/study.\n\nActually there's a simpler approach: use `defvar` + `@form` with `(set! name (input-text))`, and check in the transition: if name is empty, go back to the form step with an error message. But simplest: use form0's `form+submit` with `required` validator which gives automatic error message re-rendering. That requires `conscript/form0` require. Let's check `defvar` semantics and the `form` render approach. The doc example at line ~745 shows form+submit. And `@rw[\"name\" (input-text \"Name\")]` render style.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```racket\n#lang conscript\n\n(require conscript/form0)\n\n(provide benchmark-study)\n\n(defvar display-name)\n\n(defstep (intro)\n  @md{# Welcome\n      This is a short study. Click Continue to begin.\n      @button{Continue}})\n\n(define-values (name-form on-submit)\n  (form+submit\n   [display-name (ensure binding/text (required))]))\n\n(defstep (get-name)\n  (define (render rw)\n    @md{# Your Name\n        @rw[\"display-name\" (input-text \"Display name:\")]\n        @|submit-button|})\n  @md{@form[name-form on-submit render]})\n\n(defstep (final)\n  @md{# Thank You\n      Thanks, @display-name, for participating.})\n\n(defstudy benchmark-study\n  [intro --> get-name --> final]\n  [final --> final])\n```\n\nWait — `required` validator: does it treat empty string as missing? In forms library, `required` ensures value is present (not #f). An empty text input submits e